#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175699 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "12dc1c50-69bc-54d8-8d60-a7fc144c9644" :question/math-academy-id "e-11008" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the graph of $y=\\csc x?$\n\n- The function takes the value $1$ at $x = -\\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n,$ where $n$ is an integer\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n\n- The function has zeroes at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$" :question/worked-solution "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\csc x.$\n\n![](images/a9/a92d46319d381f0a8cab7823021b3b7e9b5c030ad964586b8e72129a2cd5abe7.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x = \\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n.$ Therefore, statement I is false.\n\n- The range of $y = \\csc x$ is $(-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty).$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The cosecant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050172}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517097 :question-generator/id #uuid "af56d31f-d222-5065-9b5a-73cf912ce6bb" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186176053.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c5e2592-4591-5762-bcbe-2782ea0f3180" :answer/typ